<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elias Howe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Elias Howe Jr.</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elias Howe Jr. was an American inventor, born in Spencer, Massachusetts, best known for creating the modern lockstitch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sewing-machine">sewing machine</a>. On 10 September 1846, he received the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> patent for a sewing machine using a lockstitch design, with a needle with the eye at the point, a shuttle beneath the cloth and an automatic feed. He defended his patent in a court case from 1849 to 1854 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-singer">Isaac Singer</a> and won considerable royalties. His machine won a gold medal at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Exhibition of 1867, the year he received the Légion d'honneur, and he died that year at age 48.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elias-howe.jpg" alt="Elias Howe" width="330" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Southwarth and Hawes; derivative work by TheCuriousGnome · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elias_Howe_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Elias Howe Jr. was the son of Dr Elias Howe Sr and Polly Bemis Howe. He grew up in Massachusetts and from 1835 apprenticed in a textile factory in Lowell. After mills closed in the Panic of 1837 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> to work as a mechanic with carding machinery, together with his cousin Nathaniel P. Banks. At the beginning of 1838 he apprenticed in the shop of Ari Davis, a master mechanic who made and repaired chronometers and other precision instruments, and it was in Davis's employ that he seized upon the idea of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sewing-machine">sewing machine</a>. He married Elizabeth Jennings Ames on 3 March 1841; they had three children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Howe was not the first to conceive of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sewing-machine">sewing machine</a>. One idea dates from as early as 1790, and others had patented designs and produced working machines, in one case at least 80. Howe was awarded the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> patent for a lockstitch machine on 10 September 1846, but had difficulty finding investors in the United States, so his elder brother Amasa Bemis Howe went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in October 1846 and sold the first machine for £250 to William Thomas of Cheapside, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, who owned a factory making corsets, umbrellas and valises.</p><p>Elias and his family joined Amasa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1848, but after business disputes with Thomas and his wife's failing health, Howe returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> nearly penniless. His wife died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> shortly after his return in 1849. He then found that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-singer">Isaac Singer</a>, with the cooperation of Walter Hunt, was selling a facsimile with the same lockstitch, and he defended his patent in a case that lasted from 1849 to 1854. In 1851 he received a patent for an Automatic, Continuous Clothing Closure, a forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zipper">zipper</a>, but did not seriously market it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Howe gave much of his earnings to equip the 17th Connecticut Volunteer Infantry of the Union Army, in which he served as a private from 14 August 1862 to 19 July 1865, acting as regimental postmaster. Between 1865 and 1867 he established the Howe Machine Co. in Bridgeport, Connecticut. His machine won a gold medal at the Paris Exhibition of 1867, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> awarded him the Légion d'honneur that year. He died on 3 October 1867 of gout and a massive blood clot and was buried in Green-Wood Cemetery in Brooklyn. He was commemorated with a 5-cent stamp in 1940 and inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elias Howe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Elias Howe</b> received a US patent for a machine using an eye-pointed needle and a shuttle to form a lockstitch.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-10">10 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
